::GEO_U05_L06_GP01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U05_L06_GP01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.7<br><br>Question:<br>A sector in diagram 1 has radius 6 and central angle 60&deg;. Which expression gives its arc length? {
=A. <sup>60</sup>&frasl;<sub>360</sub>(2&pi;6)#Arc length is the fraction of the circumference determined by the central angle: <sup>&theta;</sup>&frasl;<sub>360</sub>(2&pi;r).
~B. <sup>60</sup>&frasl;<sub>360</sub>(&pi;6<sup>2</sup>)#This is the sector area structure, not arc length.
~C. 2&pi;6#This is the full circumference, not the arc for a 60&deg; sector.
~D. &pi;6<sup>2</sup>#This is the full circle area, not an arc length.
}

::GEO_U05_L06_GP02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U05_L06_GP02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.7<br><br>Question:<br>A sector in diagram 2 has radius 8 and central angle 90&deg;. Which expression gives its sector area? {
~A. <sup>90</sup>&frasl;<sub>360</sub>(2&pi;8)#This is the arc length structure, not sector area.
=B. <sup>90</sup>&frasl;<sub>360</sub>(&pi;8<sup>2</sup>)#Sector area is the central-angle fraction of the circle area.
~C. 2&pi;8#This is circumference, not area.
~D. 90&pi;#This ignores the radius and the fraction of the circle.
}

::GEO_U05_L06_GP03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U05_L06_GP03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.7<br><br>Question:<br>A 120&deg; arc has radius 9. What coefficient of &pi; gives the arc length? {
~A. 9&pi;#This uses the radius but not the central-angle fraction.
~B. 120&pi;#This uses the angle as a length coefficient.
=C. 6&pi;#Use <sup>120</sup>&frasl;<sub>360</sub>(2&pi;9), so the coefficient of &pi; is 6.
~D. Cannot be determined#The radius and central angle are given, so the arc length coefficient can be determined.
}

::GEO_U05_L06_GP04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U05_L06_GP04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.7<br><br>Question:<br>A 45&deg; sector has radius 10. What coefficient of &pi; gives the sector area? {
~A. 10&pi;#This uses the radius but does not square it or use the angle fraction.
~B. 45&pi;#This uses the angle as an area coefficient.
~C. 2.5&pi;#This coefficient comes from arc length, not sector area.
=D. 12.5&pi;#Use <sup>45</sup>&frasl;<sub>360</sub>(&pi;10<sup>2</sup>), so the coefficient of &pi; is 12.5.
}

::GEO_U05_L06_GP05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U05_L06_GP05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.7<br><br>Question:<br>A sector in diagram 5 has radius 5 and central angle 72&deg;. Which expression gives its arc length? {
=A. <sup>72</sup>&frasl;<sub>360</sub>(2&pi;5)#Arc length is the fraction of the circumference determined by the central angle: <sup>&theta;</sup>&frasl;<sub>360</sub>(2&pi;r).
~B. <sup>72</sup>&frasl;<sub>360</sub>(&pi;5<sup>2</sup>)#This is the sector area structure, not arc length.
~C. 2&pi;5#This is the full circumference, not the arc for a 72&deg; sector.
~D. &pi;5<sup>2</sup>#This is the full circle area, not an arc length.
}

